{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"explain-vulnerability-class","kind":"prompt","title":"Explain a vulnerability class","description":"Teaches one vulnerability class, such as XSS, SQL injection, CSRF, SSRF, IDOR or path traversal, with a vulnerable snippet, how it is exploited, the fix and how to spot it in code review.","category":"learning","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["learn"],"role":["software-engineer","security-engineer","student"],"stack":[],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["topic","text"],"output":["explanation","code","checklist"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"standard","interaction":"one-shot","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","depth":"specific","tags":["owasp","secure-coding","web-vulnerabilities","code-review-skills"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["fix-xss-vulnerabilities","fix-ssrf-vulnerability","fix-idor-vulnerabilities","review-pr-for-security"]},"args":[{"name":"vulnerability","description":"The class to learn, such as \"stored XSS\", \"SQL injection\", \"CSRF\", \"SSRF\", \"IDOR\", \"path traversal\", \"insecure deserialisation\".","type":"string","required":true},{"name":"framework","description":"Your language and framework, so examples match the code you write.","type":"string"},{"name":"code","description":"Optional code of your own to look at with this vulnerability in mind.","type":"text"}],"output_contract":{"format":"markdown","sections":["What it is","Vulnerable example","How it is exploited","The fix","Spotting it in review","Practise safely"]},"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"<context>\nDevelopers learn vulnerability classes best from their own stack: the same SQL injection looks different in raw JDBC, Django's ORM `extra()` and a Node template string. Each class has a root cause that names the real fix: injection is data treated as code, fixed by keeping them separate (parameterised queries, context-aware output encoding), not by blocklists; CSRF is the browser sending credentials automatically, fixed by SameSite cookies and tokens; SSRF is the server fetching an attacker-chosen URL, which allowlists fix and blocklists fail at because of redirects, DNS rebinding and alternative IP notations; IDOR is a missing ownership check, which no input validation fixes. Frameworks protect by default until someone bypasses them (`dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, `|safe`, `raw()`, string-built queries), so the bypasses are what to look for in review.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nTeach the vulnerability class: {{vulnerability}}{{#framework}}, using {{framework}}{{/framework}}.\n{{#code}}\nThen look at the learner's code with this class in mind:\n\n```\n{{code}}\n```\n{{/code}}\n\n1. Explain what it is in two or three sentences: the root cause as a principle, where it occurs, and the realistic impact.\n2. Show a short, realistic vulnerable snippet in the learner's language and framework (or a common web stack if none was given, and say which), the kind of code that ships in real projects, not a strawman.\n3. Explain how it is exploited at a conceptual level: what the attacker controls, what input shape they send, and what happens as a result. Keep payloads to well-known textbook examples that demonstrate the idea.\n4. Show the fix in the same language and framework, explain why it addresses the root cause, and name the tempting fixes that do not work (blocklists, client-side validation, escaping in the wrong context).\n5. Give a review checklist: the code patterns, APIs and framework bypasses that signal this class, and the questions a reviewer should ask.\n6. If the learner's code was given, apply the checklist to it and say where it is or is not vulnerable, citing lines.\n7. Suggest safe places to practise: deliberately vulnerable training apps run locally, and the OWASP cheat sheet for this class.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Teach defence. Do not produce weaponised exploits, evasion techniques for real products, or steps to attack systems the learner does not own.\n- Use only well-known demonstration payloads, and say that testing is allowed only on systems you own or have written permission to test.\n- Name the real fix, not a partial mitigation, and say when defence in depth adds to it.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## What it is\nTwo or three sentences.\n## Vulnerable example\nA code block.\n## How it is exploited\nNumbered steps at a conceptual level.\n## The fix\nA code block, why it works, and fixes that do not work.\n## Spotting it in review\nA checklist, then findings on the learner's code if given.\n## Practise safely\nTwo or three suggestions.\n</output_format>","steps":[]}
